          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The responses identify barley chiefly with humble or inexpensive provision and with the early firstfruits harvest typologically linked to Christ’s resurrection, while noting that this symbolism is not universally settled.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book combines the same associations: barley is a humble spring firstfruits grain whose waved sheaf prefigures the risen Christ as firstfruits.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Barley signifies humble sustenance and, as the early firstfruits grain, typologically signifies resurrection—especially Christ as firstfruits.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Barley is the humble firstfruits grain whose offered sheaf points to Christ risen as firstfruits.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book selects and combines the two principal associations recognized across the consensus responses rather than addressing the less prominent contextual meanings of barley.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s identification is explicitly represented as a primary or common interpretation in the blind responses.\",\"rationale\":\"The minimum core is the same in both readings: barley’s early harvest and firstfruits role is connected typologically to Christ’s resurrection, alongside an association with humility or lowliness. Differences concern emphasis and the consensus caveat that barley lacks a universally settled meaning, not the symbolic identification itself.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited verses establish that barley ripens early, can be presented as firstfruits, and that Christ is called firstfruits, but Leviticus 23:10 does not explicitly identify the waved sheaf as barley, and none of the excerpts directly states that the sheaf symbolizes Christ or that barley is humble. Those conclusions require typological and historical synthesis.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
